Preamble: Two lasers generate radiation of (1) $9.5 \mu {m}$ and (2) $0.1 \mu {m}$ respectively.

Subproblem 0: Determine the photon energy (in eV, to two decimal places) of the laser generating radiation of $9.5 \mu {m}$.


Solution: \[
\begin{aligned}
{E} &={h} v=\frac{{hc}}{\lambda} {J} \times \frac{1 {eV}}{1.6 \times 10^{-19} {~J}} \\
{E}_{1} &=\frac{{hc}}{9.5 \times 10^{-6}} \times \frac{1}{1.6 \times 10^{-19}} {eV}= \boxed{0.13} {eV}
\end{aligned}
\]

Final answer: The final answer is 0.13. I hope it is correct.

Subproblem 1: Determine the photon energy (in eV, to one decimal place) of the laser generating radiation of $0.1 \mu {m}$.

generated_responses:
We are given the wavelength of the radiation from the two lasers as $\lambda_1 = 9.5 \mu m$ and $\lambda_2 = 0.1 \mu m$. We want to find the photon energy for each laser.

The photon energy is given by the equation $E = \frac{hc}{\lambda}$, where $h$ is Planck's constant ($6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}$), $c$ is the speed of light ($3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}$), and $\lambda$ is the wavelength in meters.

For the first laser with $\lambda_1 = 9.5 \mu m = 9.5 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}$:
\begin{align*} E_1 &= \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} = \frac{(6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s})(3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s})}{9.5 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}} \\ &= \frac{1.9878 \times 10^{-25} \text{ J m}}{9.5 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m}} \\ &= 2.092 \times 10^{-20} \text{ J} \end{align*}
To convert this to eV, we use the conversion factor $1 \text{ eV} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J}$:
\begin{align*} E_1 &= \frac{2.092 \times 10^{-20} \text{ J}}{1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J/eV}} \\ &= 0.1304 \text{ eV} \end{align*}
Rounding to two decimal places, we get $E_1 = 0.13 \text{ eV}$.

For the second laser with $\lambda_2 = 0.1 \mu m = 0.1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m} = 1 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}$:
\begin{align*} E_2 &= \frac{hc}{\lambda_2} = \frac{(6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s})(3 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s})}{1 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}} \\ &= \frac{1.9878 \times 10^{-25} \text{ J m}}{1 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m}} \\ &= 1.9878 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J} \end{align*}
To convert this to eV, we use the conversion factor $1 \text{ eV} = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J}$:
\begin{align*} E_2 &= \frac{1.9878 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J}}{1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J/eV}} \\ &= 12.40 \text{ eV} \end{align*}
Rounding to one decimal place, we get $E_2 = 12.4 \text{ eV}$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{0.13}$ and $\boxed{12.4}$

